---
/**
 * PILLAR PAGE — heat safety.
 *
 * This is the highest-value cluster on the site and the reason it exists as a
 * pillar rather than a blog post. Heat-illness searches are made by people who
 * need to DO something — recognise a symptom, decide whether to stop work,
 * plan a shift — and those intents require opening a page. Google cannot
 * answer them in a SERP snippet the way it answers "what is a cooling towel".
 *
 * It also carries the site's duty of care. The product is comfort equipment.
 * If someone arrives here with a heat-stroke symptom, the page must send them
 * to emergency care before it mentions a towel, and it does.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const cluster = (await getCollection('guides', ({ data }) => data.pillar === 'heat' && !data.noindex))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.published.localeCompare(a.data.published));

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between heat exhaustion and heat stroke?',
    a: 'Heat exhaustion means the body is struggling but still coping: heavy sweating, cool clammy skin, dizziness, headache, nausea, cramps. Heat stroke means thermoregulation has failed: confusion, slurred speech, loss of coordination, seizure or unconsciousness, and often hot skin that has stopped sweating. Heat exhaustion is managed with rest, shade and fluids. Heat stroke is a medical emergency — call 999 or 911 immediately and start cooling while you wait.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the warning signs I should stop work immediately?',
    a: 'Confusion or unusual behaviour, stopping sweating in the heat, vomiting, fainting, or a headache that does not ease with rest and water. Any one of these means stop, get into shade, start cooling and get help. Cramps and heavy sweating alone mean take a proper break — not push through it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much should I drink when working in heat?',
    a: 'The commonly used guidance for sustained work in heat is roughly a cup of water every 15 to 20 minutes, taken steadily rather than in large amounts at once, and not more than about a litre an hour. Drinking to thirst alone tends to under-replace during hard work. If you are sweating heavily for several hours, plain water alone can also leave you short on electrolytes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does acclimatisation actually matter?',
    a: 'It matters more than any piece of equipment. Most serious heat illness at work happens in the first few days on the job or in the first days of a heatwave, before the body has adapted. Adaptation takes roughly one to two weeks of graded exposure, and it is lost again after about a week away. New starters and people returning from leave are the highest-risk group on any site.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does a cooling towel fit in?',
    a: 'Last. Shade, water, rest breaks, pacing and acclimatisation do the real work of preventing heat illness. A cooling towel is comfort equipment that makes a hot shift more bearable and helps you cool down faster on a break. It is a genuine help and it is not protection — it will not make an unsafe shift safe.',
  },
];

const title = 'Working Safely in Heat';
const description =
  'How to recognise heat exhaustion and heat stroke, what actually prevents them on a hot shift, and where cooling gear genuinely helps.';
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="/heat-safety"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Heat safety', href: '/heat-safety' }]}
  faq={faq}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pillar guide</p>
      <h1>Working safely in heat</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Written for people who work outside and cannot simply go indoors. What
        the warning signs actually look like, what genuinely reduces risk, and
        an honest account of how little a cooling towel contributes to the
        first two.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <div class="note note-vet">
        <span class="note-title">If someone is confused, collapsed or has stopped sweating</span>
        <p>
          That is suspected heat stroke and it is a medical emergency. Call 999
          in the UK or 911 in the US now. While you wait: move them into shade,
          get their outer clothing off, and cool them aggressively — cold water
          over the skin, ice packs to the neck, armpits and groin, and air
          movement across them. Do not wait to see whether they improve.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section aria-labelledby="recognise">
        <h2 id="recognise">Recognising the two stages</h2>
        <p>
          Heat illness is a progression, not a single event, and the point of
          knowing the stages is to intervene at the first one. By the time
          someone shows the signs in the right-hand column, the situation has
          already become an emergency.
        </p>

        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption class="sr-only">Comparison of heat exhaustion and heat stroke signs</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Sign</th>
                <th scope="col">Heat exhaustion</th>
                <th scope="col">Heat stroke</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Mental state</th><td>Tired, irritable, headache</td><td>Confused, slurring, aggressive or unconscious</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Skin</th><td>Pale, cool, clammy</td><td>Hot, often red, may be dry</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Sweating</th><td>Heavy</td><td>May have stopped</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Pulse</th><td>Fast and weak</td><td>Fast and strong</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Other</th><td>Cramps, nausea, dizziness</td><td>Vomiting, seizure, collapse</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">What to do</th><td>Stop, shade, cool, fluids, monitor</td><td>Emergency services now, cool aggressively</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The single most useful thing on this table is the mental-state row.
          People are poor at judging their own heat strain, so the reliable
          signal is usually somebody else noticing that a colleague has gone
          quiet, is repeating themselves, or is behaving out of character.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="prevent">
        <h2 id="prevent">What actually prevents it</h2>
        <p>
          In rough order of how much difference each one makes:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Acclimatisation.</strong> Build exposure over one to two
            weeks. Most serious cases happen in the first few days, to new
            starters and to people back from leave.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Rest in genuine shade.</strong> Shade that is still hot and
            airless barely counts. Air movement is doing at least half the work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Steady fluid intake.</strong> Roughly a cup every 15 to 20
            minutes during sustained work, with electrolytes over long, heavily
            sweating days.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pacing and scheduling.</strong> Moving the hardest work to
            the early morning removes more risk than any product can.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Watching each other.</strong> The buddy check is what
            catches confusion before collapse.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cooling equipment.</strong> Helpful, genuinely pleasant, and
            last on this list for a reason.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="note note-warn">
          <span class="note-title">Know the number that matters</span>
          <p>
            Air temperature alone is a poor predictor of heat strain, because
            humidity determines whether your sweat can evaporate at all. The
            <a href="/tools/heat-index">heat risk calculator</a> combines the
            two into the heat index and tells you which risk band you are
            working in.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="gear">
        <h2 id="gear">Where cooling gear genuinely helps</h2>
        <p>
          It helps most on breaks. Actively cooling during a rest period gets
          your core temperature down faster than passive rest in shade, which
          means the break does more and you start the next hour in better shape.
          Worn during work, an evaporative towel on the neck makes heat more
          tolerable, which has real value even though it is not protection.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it will not do is extend how long you can safely work. If the
          only reason a shift feels manageable is the towel, the shift is the
          problem. See <a href="/cooling-towels">how cooling towels work</a> for
          the mechanism and its limits, particularly in humid heat.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>

    {cluster.length > 0 && (
      <section aria-labelledby="cluster">
        <h2 id="cluster">Guides in this cluster</h2>
        <ul class="cluster-list">
          {cluster.map((g) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`/guides/${g.id}`}>{g.data.title}</a>
              <span class="muted"> — {g.data.description}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    )}

    <div class="prose">
      <FaqList items={faq} />

      <div class="note">
        <span class="note-title">Sources and scope</span>
        <p>
          This page summarises widely published occupational-health guidance on
          heat illness for a general audience. It is not medical advice and it
          does not replace your employer's heat plan or your own risk
          assessment. In the UK, HSE publishes guidance on working in heat; in
          the US, OSHA and NIOSH do. Read those for the regulatory position that
          applies to your site.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cluster-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .cluster-list li { padding: var(--s3) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .cluster-list a { font-weight: 650; }
  .prose ol { padding-left: 1.25rem; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .prose ol li { margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  table caption { text-align: left; }
</style>
